\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Plot of the Semi-Circle.}}{22}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Plot of $F_{(2)}(x)$.}}{24}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Plot of a simulation of a Coulomb gas. In black crosses we plotted one sample from the simulation. In shadowy dots, any \enquote {averaged} samples indicating the equilibrium region.}}{25}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces For all graphs, we have in dashed line the Semicircle distribution. In black the average distribution of eigenvalues is displayed. The columns correspond to $n = 10,50,100$ from left to right. The lines correspond to the GOE, GUE and GSE from top to bottom. The matrices, and corresponding eigenvalues, were sampled with the ensemble distribution $10000$ for each graph.}}{34}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces For all graphs, the line is the average distribution of eigenvalues for the GUE. The columns correspond to the polynomial order (and matrix size) $n = 10,50,100$ from left to right. The dashes in the axis correspond to the roots of the $n$-th Hermite polynomial.}}{36}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Plots of $F_{TW}^{(\beta )}(t)$ for $\beta = 1, 2, 4$.}}{53}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces $20$ Non-intersecting Brownian paths for $0 < t < 1$ s.t. they start and end at $y=0.$}}{54}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Three conditions simulated form the $20$ Dyson Bridges taken from $t = 0.1, 0.5, 0.9$. For each one the experimental density (line plot) and one sampled position (dashes in axis) were plotted. The three plots are re-scaled by $((2t(1-t))\sqrt {N}).$}}{55}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces In the Plot it is represented the plot for $\ee ^{- \lambda (\cosh (x)-1)}$ and an increasing $\lambda = 1, 2, 10, 70$ - darker represent larger values of the parameter. The dotted patter represents $\ee ^{-\lambda x^2}$ for large lambda.}}{61}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The lines represent the solution to $\mathop {\textrm Im}[f(z) - f(t_c)] = 0$ for the three possible cases of the Hermite Polynomial. These are the curves we use to perform the asymptotic analysis on the integral. Note the case $\alpha = 2$; the coalescence of critical point gives origin to the Airy function.}}{73}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Shown in the shadow is the mean configurations - in some an approximation of the limit distribution of points, and in the scatter is a random $n = 50$ sample from the distribution. We show configurations for combinations of the parameters $t = 0, 0.5, 1, 2$ from left ro right and $a = 2, 3, 5$ from top to bottom.}}{77}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Four conditions for the Monic potential, the equilibrium measure is expressed by the line plot. For all cases we used $t=1$ and set $m=1,2,3,5$ from left to right. The bin-plot is the result of the simulation of the equivalent Coulomb Gas taken from \citeonline {TCCJoaoPimenta}. }}{82}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces The figure depicts an annulus referring to the equilibrium measure when $r_0 > 0$. As the complex plane integral is reduced to a line integral, we draw the domain change and the point of interest $r_\tau $ where $V$ has a critical point. Around this point we depict the region around the minima, which in the method that should account for the main order of the integral we solve for.}}{95}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces The figure depicts an disk referring to the equilibrium measure when $r_0 = 0$. As the complex plane integral is reduced to a line integral, we draw the domain change and the point of interest $r_\tau $ where $V$ has a critical point. Around this point we depict the region around the minima, which in the method that should account for the main order of the integral we solve for. The left image corresponds to the case when $r_{j/N} >> N^{-\epsilon }$ and the right image corresponds to $r_{j/N} << N^{-\epsilon }$. In this case $r*_{\tau }$ will define the contributing region to the integral.}}{106}{figure.caption.32}%
